A subcommand typed after global flags (yoyo --yes doctor, yoyo --model M status) is now refused with exit 2 instead of being sent to the model as a paid prompt. Build, clippy, fmt and the full test suite pass, and it's committed as dd66eb73.

The bug, reproduced first: on the built binary, yoyo --yes doctor and yoyo --model claude-haiku-5-5 status both printed yoyo (prompt mode) and sent the words to the model as a prompt.

The fix:
• `src/cli.rs`: KNOWN_SUBCOMMANDS is now one module-level list. A new misplaced_subcommand(args) finds the first word that isn't a flag. It skips the values of flags that take one and stops at --. If that word is a subcommand but not args[1], it's a match. misplaced_subcommand_refusal builds the message from the real command line.
• `src/dispatch_sub.rs`: before dispatching, a match prints the refusal to stderr and exits 2.

Two changes from the plan:
1. The message doesn't move the flags after the subcommand. I ran the suggested forms first:
   • yoyo doctor --model M uses the model.
   • status and config get model ignore --model.
   • yoyo run echo hi --model M printed hi --model M, so the moved flag ended up inside the user's command.

   So the refusal says subcommands go first: yoyo doctor and that the flags before it were not applied. Its second suggestion sends the words as a prompt: yoyo --model M -p doctor.
2. Seven subcommands were missing from the list: ast, def, gasp, goal, model, risk and security. Without them, yoyo --yes goal would still have become a prompt. I added them, and a new test fails if any subcommand handled in dispatch_sub.rs is left out of the list.

Tests:
• A table test for the new check, covering --, --model doctor (where "doctor" is the model name) and -p doctor.
• A test of the exact refusal text.
• Real-binary tests in tests/integration.rs, using the file's existing helper that strips API keys. They run with a bad key, an unreachable base URL and a temp directory, and kill any prompt-mode run as soon as it starts, so a regression fails fast.
  • Refused with exit 2: the four shapes from the plan.
  • Still prompts: the four near-misses, such as yoyo -p doctor and yoyo --yes explain this code.
  • Unchanged: yoyo doctor still runs doctor.
• I disabled the check, ran the tests and restored it in one command. Only the refusal test went red; after restoring, everything was green.
• cli.rs first went over its size cap. Moving the two tests about the dispatcher into dispatch_sub.rs brought both files back under their limits: cli.rs is at 7869 and dispatch_sub.rs at 2596.

I also added a short note to docs/src/usage/commands.md.

Next slice, not filed: actually running the subcommand after global flags, which would need each subcommand to handle those flags itself. No issue tracks this task, so I haven't recorded that anywhere yet.
